(a) Every pawnbroker shall make a daily report in writing to the chief of police, in such form as may be prescribed by the chief, of all property pledged to or brought by him during the 24 hours ending at 8:00 p.m. on the date of the report. Such report shall be typewritten. In addition to any other information required by the chief of police, they shall show:
(1) The name and address of the pawnbroker;
(2) The time of each and every transaction;
(3) Serial number of pawn tickets;
(4) Amount paid or advanced for each transaction; and
(5) Full description of articles, including kind, style, material, color, design, kind and number of stones in jewelry, together with all identifying names, marks, and numbers thereon.
(b) Insufficient reports shall be rejected and any pawnbroker making such shall be deemed guilty of a violation of this section.
(Code 1977, § 8-4011(g))

662:
The following words, terms, or phrases, when used in this section, shall have the meaning ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
(1) Amusement game room means any location as provided in O.C.G.A. § 16-12-35(b), (c) or (d) where one or more bona fide coin-operated amusement machines are operated that permit non-cash redemption as provided in O.C.G.A. § 16-12-35(d)(1)(B),(C), or a combination thereof.
(2) Bona fide coin-operated amusement machine means the same as this term is defined in O.C.G.A. § 50-27-70(b)(2)(A) and (B) and any applicable regulations of the state. Examples of bona fide coin-operated amusement machines include, but are expressly not limited to, the following:
a. Pinball machines;
b. Console machines;
c. Video games;
d. Crane machines;
e. Claw machines;
f. Pusher machines;
g. Bowling machines;
h. Novelty arcade games;
i. Foosball or table soccer machines;
j. Miniature racetrack, football or golf machines;
k. Target or shooting gallery machines;
l. Basketball machines;
m. Shuffleboard machines;
n. Kiddie ride games;
o. Skee-ball ® machines;
p. Air hockey machines;
q. Roll down machines;
r. Coin-operated pool table or coin-operated billiard table as defined in paragraph (3) of O.C.G.A. § 43-8-1; and
s. Any other similar amusement machine which can be legally operated in the state.
(3) The term bona fide coin-operated amusement machine does not include the following:
a. Coin-operated washing machines or dryers;
b. Vending machines which for payment of money dispense products or services;
c. Gas and electric meters;
d. Pay telephones;
e. Pay toilets;
f. Cigarette vending machines;
g. Coin-operated vending machines;
h. Coin-operated scales;
i. Coin-operated gumball machines;
j. Coin-operated television sets which provide cable or network programming;
k. Coin-operated massage beds; and
l. Machines which are not legally permitted to be operated in the state.
(4) Location means a business within the city that has complied with the provisions of the ordinances of the city relating to occupation taxes and business licenses.
(Ord. No. 2013-46, § 1, 7-23-13)

664:
Application for a license for operating an amusement game room within the corporate limits of the city shall be filed in writing with the occupation tax administrator, on a form to be provided by the city, and shall include:
(1) Name, address, and age of the applicant and the date of the application and, if a firm, corporation, partnership or association, the principal officers thereof and their addresses;
(2) Address or place where the licensed bona fide coin-operated amusement machine or machines are to be offered to the public for play and the other business or businesses operated at that place or places;
(3) The number of machines to be maintained on the premises and the type of machines to be maintained;
(4) Name and address of the owner of the machine or machines and a copy of the owner's master license;
(5) Whether or not the person or persons having management of or supervision over the applicant's business have been convicted of a crime, misdemeanor or the violation of any municipal ordinance relating to narcotics or any crime of moral turpitude, the nature of such offense, and the punishment assessed therefore;
(6) Name and address of any other business owned or operated by applicant within the corporate limits of the city;
(7) List of any other licenses or permits from the city held by the applicant.
Upon issuing a license for an amusement game room, the city official or employee shall provide the license with a copy of this section. A license issued in accordance with this section shall be valid until December 31 of the year in which the license was issued. The owner or operator of an amusement game room shall be required to pay occupation taxes in accordance with title 3, chapter 2 of this Code.
(Ord. No. 2013-46, § 1, 7-23-13)

